task.description:
We currently run fraud checks in n8n with this workflow:

```json
{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "name": "Webhook - Transaction",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.webhook",
      "position": [250, 300]
    },
    {
      "name": "Postgres - Get Account",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.postgres",
      "position": [450, 300],
      "parameters": {
        "query": "SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE account_id = {{ $json.account_id }}"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "Postgres - Historical Fraud",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.postgres",
      "position": [650, 300],
      "parameters": {
        "query": "SELECT COUNT(*) as fraud_count FROM transactions WHERE account_id = {{ $json.account_id }} AND is_fraud = true"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "OpenAI - Fraud Analysis",
      "type": "@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.lmChatOpenAi",
      "position": [850, 300],
      "parameters": {
        "prompt": "Analyze this transaction for fraud: amount={{ $json.amount }}, merchant={{ $json.merchant_name }}, account_fraud_history={{ $json.fraud_count }}. Return JSON with fraud_score (0-1) and reasoning."
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "IF - Block Decision",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.if",
      "position": [1050, 300],
      "parameters": {
        "conditions": {
          "number": [
            {
              "value1": "={{ $json.fraud_score }}",
              "operation": "larger",
              "value2": 0.7
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "Postgres - Update Blacklist",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.postgres",
      "position": [1250, 200],
      "parameters": {
        "query": "UPDATE accounts SET blacklisted = true WHERE account_id = {{ $json.account_id }}"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "HTTP - Block Response",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.respondToWebhook",
      "position": [1450, 200],
      "parameters": {
        "response": "BLOCK"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "HTTP - Approve Response",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.respondToWebhook",
      "position": [1450, 400],
      "parameters": {
        "response": "APPROVE"
      }
    }
  ],
  "connections": {
    "Webhook - Transaction": { "main": [[{ "node": "Postgres - Get Account" }]] },
    "Postgres - Get Account": { "main": [[{ "node": "Postgres - Historical Fraud" }]] },
    "Postgres - Historical Fraud": { "main": [[{ "node": "OpenAI - Fraud Analysis" }]] },
    "OpenAI - Fraud Analysis": { "main": [[{ "node": "IF - Block Decision" }]] },
    "IF - Block Decision": {
      "main": [
        [{ "node": "Postgres - Update Blacklist" }],
        [{ "node": "HTTP - Approve Response" }]
      ]
    },
    "Postgres - Update Blacklist": { "main": [[{ "node": "HTTP - Block Response" }]] }
  }
}
```

This works but we're seeing:
1. High false-positive rate (good customers blocked)
2. LLM calls add 800-1200ms latency
3. Fraudsters seem to adapt around our thresholds within days
4. The historical fraud count query looks at ALL history, not just recent patterns
5. We have no idea if the LLM scores are calibrated or drifting

Build a production-grade fraud decisioning pipeline that scores transactions in <100ms, uses point-in-time velocity features, trains a proper model on chargeback labels (respecting their 45-day maturity window), calibrates decision thresholds to our $4:$1 false-decline-to-fraud cost ratio, and monitors for adversarial drift. We have transaction events streaming into Kafka, and reference tables (accounts, devices, merchants) in Postgres.

